    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Maternal and child health in Iran connects care before and during pregnancy with childbirth, newborn services and continuing childhood prevention. National child-survival estimates show improvement, while UNICEF identifies persistent geographical and socioeconomic inequalities in newborn outcomes.",
    "tags": ["Maternal health", "Child survival", "Newborn care", "Midwifery", "Health equity"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:36:30Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:36:30Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Maternal and Child Health in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore maternal and newborn care in Iran, child-survival trends, regional inequalities and evidence on continuity of midwifery care."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "continuum-of-care",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A connected sequence of care",
        "content": "Maternal and child health brings together services that are often discussed separately: support before pregnancy, antenatal care during pregnancy, childbirth services, newborn care and continuing childhood prevention. WHO’s global position paper on midwifery models emphasizes continuity, respectful care and collaboration across professions. This is an international framework for understanding service design, rather than evidence that a particular model operates throughout Iran. [s4]\n\nUNICEF’s Iran programme describes work with the Ministry of Health and Medical Education and affiliated medical universities. Priorities include strengthening primary care in disadvantaged areas, improving newborn-care capacity, supporting breastfeeding and maintaining the vaccine cold chain, the temperature-controlled system needed to preserve vaccines. These are programme activities and objectives; their description alone does not establish their effects on mortality. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "unequal-outcomes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Looking beyond the national average",
        "content": "UNICEF identifies substantial differences in newborn mortality between provinces, with particular concerns in economically disadvantaged areas and places with high concentrations of refugees. Its programme names Hormozgan, Kerman, North Khorasan, and Sistan and Baluchestan as priority provinces. A national average therefore cannot describe every family’s circumstances or every facility’s capacity. [s1]\n\nThe same programme includes assessment of neonatal intensive care units, specialized hospital services for newborns requiring intensive treatment. This illustrates why access involves both community services and referral facilities. The programme’s stated ambitions should remain distinct from verified achievements; this page does not treat its mortality targets as completed results. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "child-survival-trend",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: estimated under-five mortality, 2012–2024",
          "dataset_id": "under-five-mortality",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["under-five-rate"],
          "x_axis_label": "Gregorian calendar year",
          "y_axis_label": "Deaths before age five per 1,000 live births",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The displayed national estimates decline from 17.5 in 2012 to 11.3 in 2024. They describe a national trend and do not establish provincial equality or identify the contribution of any individual programme."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the evidence can establish",
        "content": "UNICEF’s country data display a declining under-five mortality series. These are estimates rather than a simple annual count of registered deaths. The retrieved values are reproduced below, but the live page’s release date and availability at the requested editorial cutoff were not established. The dataset therefore requires a dated release check before publication. Missing later observations are not assumed to be zero. [s2]\n\nResearch also examines the organization of maternity care. A quasi-experimental study in Ahvaz compared team midwifery care with routine care among women giving birth for the first time. It reported more vaginal births and exclusive breastfeeding in the team-care group, while finding no statistically significant difference in postpartum haemorrhage or induction of labour. Its limited settings and nonrandomized design constrain generalization. It supports further investigation of continuity of care, rather than a claim that one local study proves a nationwide effect. [s3]\n\nUnderstanding progress requires reading national trends alongside inequalities and service-level research. None of these sources alone provides a complete account of maternal safety, newborn care and childhood health across Iran. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
